{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789941707,"nonce":"X9mmAy3HGoxtXUdM5xTihCD+RpY=","title":"tiered: a stub whose address is kept answers every call through a mutex and a hash lookup","message":"A lazy function whose address something takes (a function value made\nbefore its first call, a code pointer stored in a global) has its\nsymbol defined as a stub, and the stub stays where the address went:\nevery call through it enters `lazy_compile`, which took the published\ntable's mutex and hashed the bead id before calling the entry. Lua's\nshape hooks are reached that way, and binary_trees called one 33\nmillion times: 2.5 of its 3.5 seconds.\n\nFix: the published entries also live in a lock-free table by bead id,\nread first by `lazy_compile`; a stub call is then a load and an\nindirect call. The stub itself still cannot be patched.","files":null}]}